+## 2026.11.24a
+
+**The heavy slots, a hotfix of 2026.11.23a** (HOTFIX-004; the owner's two decisions of 2026-11-24, asked in the question form: the number of heavy-job slots becomes a setting, one by default, and it ships at once as a hotfix installed mid-release): the launcher can run up to four heavy jobs at once on one computer, never two from one checkout, and the mutation engine refuses a second run of the same mutants file. Built on `release/2026.11.23a`, so this release is 2026.11.23a plus only this change. Two programs change, `bridge-run.py` and `mutation-run.py`; against 2026.11.23a, no file new or removed, no allow or deny entry changed.
+
+**What changed**
+- **The setting** (`bridge-run.py`): `~/.cursor-bridge/heavy-slots.json` holding exactly `{"slots": <n>}`, n a JSON integer from 1 to 4, read at each heavy launch without following a link, at most 4096 bytes. Absent: one slot, as 2026.11.23a, nothing printed. Anything else than that exact form: one slot and the note line `bridge-run: note - heavy-slots.json ignored (<cause>); one heavy slot` after the started line, never a refusal. `bridge-run.py --heavy-slots <n>` writes it atomically, prints `bridge-run: heavy slots set to <n>` and exits 0, starting nothing and writing no run row; out of range, not an integer, or given with a command or another option, it exits 125 and writes nothing, no temporary file left. Only the launcher's own words before `--` are read for it: a command after `--` that carries `--heavy-slots` runs as any other.
+- **The slots**: slot 1 keeps `heavy.lock` and its holder file; slot k from 2 uses `heavy-<k>.lock` and `heavy-<k>.lock.holder`, locked and published exactly as slot 1. All n lock files are opened before the first pass; an open or lock error on any of them refuses with 125 naming that file. A lock file that is a link, or a regular file with a second hard link, is refused the same way, unchanged (`bridge-run: refused - ~/.cursor-bridge/<file> (<error class>)`). With more than one slot the slot-taken line gains ` (slot <k> of <n>)`, and the waiting line is `bridge-run: waiting for a slot (<n> of <n> held): <clause>; <clause>`, one clause per slot; the wait's bound is the earliest live holder's end, fixed once, floored and capped as before, and the exit-123 line names the holder of the wait in progress. With one slot every line and exit is byte-identical to 2026.11.23a's.
+- **One heavy run per checkout**: before any slot, a heavy launch takes an OS byte lock on `~/.cursor-bridge/heavy-folder-<h>.lock`, `<h>` the SHA-256 of its checkout's top folder (the `--workspace` of the builder's command as written after `--`, else the launch folder, raised to git's top level of that folder, resolved, case-folded on Windows), and holds it to its end; its holder record is published when the slot is taken. Two worktrees may run heavy jobs at once; one checkout, top folder or subfolder, never two.
+- **Run ids**: today's form, the `-<n>` suffix where taken, made unique by creating the pid file `run/launcher/pids/<run id>` exclusively, after the launch's early refusals and just before its started line; every exit before the child starts removes it. Where `run/` exists but the pid file cannot be made (a file at `run/launcher/pids`, a permission error), the launch is refused with 125 and `bridge-run: refused - cannot reserve run/launcher/pids/<run id> (<error class>)`, never run with an id it did not reserve; 2026.11.23a ran such a launch with no pid file. A heavy run's row gains `"slot": <k>`; `"slot": "not free"` stays as before, and every reader compares only with `"not free"`. The existing test `test_r040_wait_timeout_exit_123_and_log` now expects `"slot": 1` on the successful one-slot row where it expected no `slot` key.
+- **The mutation engine** (`mutation-run.py`): before it removes or writes anything it locks `<scratch base>/.mutation-run-<run id>.lock` with the launcher's byte-lock primitive; a second run of the same run id ends at once with exit 2 and `mutation-run: refused - this mutants file is already running (<run id>); wait for it to end`, writing nothing and leaving the live run's scratch folder as it is; a lock file with a second hard link is refused the same way. The run that holds the lock removes its scratch folder before it unlocks. Runs of different mutants files share the scratch base freely.
+
+**Known limits** (recorded, not built)
+- No queue order: a waiter can lose a freed slot to another waiter, so an exit 123 is likelier with more slots; the loop's retry rule covers it.
+- Two heavy runs share the computer's CPU, so each is slower; that feeds `--limit auto`'s learned limits, `--mutant-timeout` and any timing test. Two suites of one project in two worktrees can collide on a fixed port or database.
+- A builder could write the setting file, which the boundary listing does not watch; the cap of 4 bounds it and the `(slot k of n)` suffix shows it.
+- Lowering the count while slot 2 is held leaves both runs live until the second ends.
+
+**Running projects must**
+- *(all phases)* Install with no heavy run live (no builder, test, driver or mutation run in any project), restart the app, run `/calibrate-bridge` before the next increment. On the real install the installer ends 0 with `RESULT: INSTALLED TREE MATCHES bridge 2026.11.24a`; over 2026.11.23a installed whole it also prints `previous 2026.11.23a kept at <folder>`. A 2026.11.23a launcher still running at the install would see slot 1 only and take no folder lock, which is why nothing heavy runs then.
+- *(all phases)* Nothing else is required: one slot stays the default. On a computer whose owner chose more slots, the supervisor runs `python ~/.claude/cursor-bridge/bridge-run.py --heavy-slots <n>` on the owner's recorded words only, never on its own judgement or another session's message.
+- *(building, changing)* With more than one slot, a second increment runs in its own worktree under `<root>/Worktrees/` (rule 39), never a second heavy run in one checkout. While Review B reads one branch, no commit lands on another worktree's branch: `review-guard.py verify` flags a moved local ref anywhere in the repository.
+- *(building, changing)* The loop's sentences that say one slot stay as written in this hotfix; they hold wherever the default of one holds. With more than one slot, read them with the setting: `workflow/phases/delegation-loop.md` (the paragraph naming the one heavy slot and building one increment after the other; the heavy launch's duration's "the computer's one slot"; step 3b's slot sentences naming `heavy.lock`, and the wake-timer from the exit line's `ends by`, which with more slots is the earliest holder's end), `workflow/phases/run-parameters.md` (Parallel increments, two sentences), `Glossary.md` (the heavy slot), `commands/supervisor.md` (two sentences), `bridge-run.py`'s module docstring and `loop-guard.py`'s comment on the slot. Rewording them belongs to a later release's text increment.
+
+**If rolled back**
+- *(all phases)* Roll back with no heavy run live. 2026.11.23a's launcher reads no setting and takes slot 1 only; `heavy-slots.json`, the `heavy-<k>.lock` and `heavy-folder-<h>.lock` files and the mutation engine's `.mutation-run-<run id>.lock` files are left in place, harmless and unread. Rows with an integer `slot` stay readable: every reader compares with `"not free"` only. A second worktree's increment in flight finishes after the first's, one at a time again.
+
